1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:01,973
They say the lottery is a tax

2
00:00:01,973 --> 00:00:03,810
on people who can't do maths.

3
00:00:04,493 --> 00:00:05,843
In two thousand three,

4
00:00:05,843 --> 00:00:08,168
a retired shopkeeper in a small Michigan

5
00:00:08,168 --> 00:00:09,618
town did the maths.

6
00:00:09,618 --> 00:00:10,980
And he found a lottery that was

7
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:12,368
losing money to him.

8
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:14,560
Over the next nine years,

9
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:16,535
he and his wife won almost twenty-seven

10
00:00:16,535 --> 00:00:18,385
million dollars in prizes.

11
00:00:18,385 --> 00:00:19,335
Legally.

12
00:00:20,027 --> 00:00:21,402
This is the story of Jerry and

13
00:00:21,402 --> 00:00:22,527
Marge Selbee.

14
00:00:26,318 --> 00:00:26,918
Evart,

15
00:00:26,668 --> 00:00:27,343
Michigan.

16
00:00:27,343 --> 00:00:28,243
A small town,

17
00:00:28,243 --> 00:00:29,943
a long way from anywhere.

18
00:00:30,535 --> 00:00:31,510
For seventeen years,

19
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:33,485
Jerry and Marge ran the convenience store

20
00:00:33,485 --> 00:00:34,185
there.

21
00:00:34,185 --> 00:00:34,785
Milk,

22
00:00:34,723 --> 00:00:35,323
bread,

23
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:35,810
coffee,

24
00:00:35,622 --> 00:00:36,985
and lottery tickets.

25
00:00:37,652 --> 00:00:39,852
Jerry had studied mathematics at college.

26
00:00:39,852 --> 00:00:41,064
He liked puzzles.

27
00:00:41,064 --> 00:00:41,864
Patterns.

28
00:00:41,864 --> 00:00:43,777
Numbers that didn't quite add up.

29
00:00:44,393 --> 00:00:45,718
In two thousand three,

30
00:00:45,718 --> 00:00:46,968
already retired,

31
00:00:46,968 --> 00:00:48,231
he picked up a brochure for a

32
00:00:48,231 --> 00:00:50,443
new lottery game called Winfall.

33
00:00:51,252 --> 00:00:53,277
Most lotteries work the same way.

34
00:00:53,277 --> 00:00:54,690
If nobody wins the jackpot,

35
00:00:54,690 --> 00:00:55,452
it grows.

36
00:00:55,452 --> 00:00:56,502
And grows.

37
00:00:57,152 --> 00:00:58,652
Winfall had a twist.

38
00:00:58,652 --> 00:01:00,877
If the jackpot passed five million dollars

39
00:01:00,877 --> 00:01:02,802
and nobody matched all six numbers,

40
00:01:02,802 --> 00:01:04,177
it didn't keep growing.

41
00:01:04,177 --> 00:01:05,452
It rolled down.

42
00:01:06,093 --> 00:01:07,555
All that money was shared out among

43
00:01:07,555 --> 00:01:08,943
the smaller prizes.

44
00:01:08,943 --> 00:01:10,693
The people who matched five numbers,

45
00:01:10,693 --> 00:01:11,368
or four,

46
00:01:11,368 --> 00:01:12,318
or three.

47
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:14,122
Jerry read the rules and,

48
00:01:14,122 --> 00:01:15,073
by one account,

49
00:01:15,073 --> 00:01:16,460
saw the flaw in less than two

50
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:17,210
minutes.

51
00:01:17,927 --> 00:01:19,052
On a roll-down week,

52
00:01:19,052 --> 00:01:21,002
the small prizes grew so big that

53
00:01:21,102 --> 00:01:22,127
on average,

54
00:01:22,127 --> 00:01:23,552
a ticket was worth more than the

55
00:01:23,552 --> 00:01:24,777
dollar it cost.

56
00:01:25,393 --> 00:01:26,493
Buy enough tickets,

57
00:01:26,493 --> 00:01:28,068
and the maths was on your side

58
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:30,322
Think of it like this.

59
00:01:30,322 --> 00:01:30,935
Normally,

60
00:01:30,935 --> 00:01:32,797
a lottery pays out less in prizes

61
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:33,922
than it takes in.

62
00:01:33,922 --> 00:01:35,885
That gap is the lottery's profit,

63
00:01:35,885 --> 00:01:37,960
and it's why players lose over time

64
00:01:38,752 --> 00:01:40,752
Imagine a raffle with a hundred tickets

65
00:01:40,752 --> 00:01:41,977
at a dollar each,

66
00:01:41,977 --> 00:01:43,964
and a prize of fifty dollars.

67
00:01:43,964 --> 00:01:45,077
Buy every ticket,

68
00:01:45,077 --> 00:01:46,602
and you spend a hundred dollars to

69
00:01:46,602 --> 00:01:47,677
win fifty.

70
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:50,393
Now imagine one week where the prize

71
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:52,668
suddenly jumps to a hundred and fifty

72
00:01:52,831 --> 00:01:54,118
Buy every ticket,

73
00:01:54,118 --> 00:01:55,368
and you spend a hundred to win

74
00:01:55,368 --> 00:01:56,793
a hundred and fifty.

75
00:01:57,502 --> 00:01:59,127
That was a roll-down week.

76
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:00,952
The trick was buying so many tickets

77
00:02:00,952 --> 00:02:02,227
that luck evened out,

78
00:02:02,227 --> 00:02:03,977
and the averages took over.

79
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:08,160
Jerry didn't tell Marge at first.

80
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:09,410
He drove to a store in the

81
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:10,198
next town,

82
00:02:10,198 --> 00:02:12,135
and bought two thousand two hundred dollars'

83
00:02:12,135 --> 00:02:13,335
worth of tickets.

84
00:02:13,935 --> 00:02:15,160
He checked them by hand at the

85
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:16,210
kitchen table.

86
00:02:16,210 --> 00:02:17,848
They'd won two thousand one hundred and

87
00:02:17,848 --> 00:02:18,572
fifty.

88
00:02:18,572 --> 00:02:19,910
A small loss.

89
00:02:20,502 --> 00:02:21,827
He hadn't bought enough to beat the

90
00:02:21,827 --> 00:02:22,439
luck.

91
00:02:22,439 --> 00:02:23,240
So next time,

92
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:24,177
he bought more.

93
00:02:24,177 --> 00:02:25,552
And then more again.

94
00:02:26,168 --> 00:02:27,943
He bet eight thousand dollars,

95
00:02:27,943 --> 00:02:30,243
and won fifteen thousand seven hundred.

96
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:32,335
Then he told Marge.

97
00:02:33,252 --> 00:02:34,965
Marge didn't tell him to stop.

98
00:02:34,965 --> 00:02:36,302
She was good at keeping track of

99
00:02:36,302 --> 00:02:37,102
things.

100
00:02:37,727 --> 00:02:38,939
They set up a company,

101
00:02:38,939 --> 00:02:40,777
G. S. Investment Strategies,

102
00:02:40,777 --> 00:02:42,839
and sold shares at five hundred dollars

103
00:02:42,839 --> 00:02:43,577
each.

104
00:02:43,577 --> 00:02:44,989
First to their children.

105
00:02:44,989 --> 00:02:46,727
Then to friends in Evart.

106
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,585
On roll-down weeks,

107
00:02:48,585 --> 00:02:50,598
they bought tickets by the thousand.

108
00:02:50,598 --> 00:02:51,773
Then every one of them had to

109
00:02:51,773 --> 00:02:52,373
be checked,

110
00:02:52,273 --> 00:02:52,873
by hand,

111
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:54,360
for hours on end.

112
00:02:56,527 --> 00:02:58,052
In May two thousand five,

113
00:02:58,052 --> 00:02:59,515
Michigan shut Winfall down,

114
00:02:59,515 --> 00:03:00,827
with no warning.

115
00:03:01,535 --> 00:03:02,885
But Jerry had heard of a similar

116
00:03:02,885 --> 00:03:04,310
game in Massachusetts,

117
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:05,935
called Cash WinFall.

118
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:07,410
It rolled down even sooner,

119
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:09,035
at two million dollars.

120
00:03:09,685 --> 00:03:11,648
So whenever a roll-down was coming,

121
00:03:11,648 --> 00:03:13,535
Jerry and Marge drove from Michigan to

122
00:03:13,535 --> 00:03:14,672
Massachusetts.

123
00:03:14,672 --> 00:03:16,060
Hundreds of miles.

124
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:17,710
Then they bought more tickets.

125
00:03:19,893 --> 00:03:21,118
They weren't the only ones who had

126
00:03:21,118 --> 00:03:21,968
noticed.

127
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:24,393
A group of students from the Massachusetts

128
00:03:24,393 --> 00:03:26,681
Institute of Technology had spotted the same

129
00:03:26,681 --> 00:03:27,580
flaw.

130
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:29,693
Led by a student called James Harvey

131
00:03:29,793 --> 00:03:31,293
they formed their own group,

132
00:03:31,293 --> 00:03:33,168
called Random Strategies.

133
00:03:33,818 --> 00:03:34,943
In one roll-down,

134
00:03:34,943 --> 00:03:37,006
the M. I. T. group reportedly bet

135
00:03:37,006 --> 00:03:39,793
seventeen million dollars in just seven hours

136
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:43,348
In twenty eleven,

137
00:03:43,348 --> 00:03:45,447
journalists noticed that a handful of groups

138
00:03:45,447 --> 00:03:46,760
were winning a huge share of the

139
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:47,710
prizes.

140
00:03:48,327 --> 00:03:50,977
The state's inspector general investigated.

141
00:03:50,977 --> 00:03:53,427
The Selbees were never charged with anything

142
00:03:53,527 --> 00:03:55,652
They had simply played by the rules

143
00:03:56,402 --> 00:03:59,277
Cash WinFall ended in January twenty twelve

144
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:01,460
Over nine years,

145
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:03,885
the Selbees' group won almost twenty-seven million

146
00:04:03,885 --> 00:04:05,410
dollars in prizes.

147
00:04:06,027 --> 00:04:07,977
After paying for all those tickets,

148
00:04:07,977 --> 00:04:09,990
the profit was about seven point seven

149
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:11,577
five million dollars,

150
00:04:11,577 --> 00:04:13,802
shared among everyone who'd bought in.

151
00:04:14,518 --> 00:04:16,505
Jerry and Marge stayed in Evart.

152
00:04:16,505 --> 00:04:17,568
In twenty twenty-two,

153
00:04:17,568 --> 00:04:19,781
their story became a Hollywood film,

154
00:04:19,781 --> 00:04:21,593
Jerry and Marge Go Large.

155
00:04:22,402 --> 00:04:23,927
They didn't beat the lottery.

156
00:04:23,927 --> 00:04:25,665
They read the rules more carefully than

157
00:04:25,665 --> 00:04:27,127
the people who wrote them.

158
00:04:27,818 --> 00:04:28,468
Most weeks,

159
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:30,193
the lottery still wins.

160
00:04:30,193 --> 00:04:31,156
But for a few days at a

161
00:04:31,156 --> 00:04:31,756
time,

162
00:04:31,743 --> 00:04:33,618
the lottery was beating itself.

163
00:04:33,618 --> 00:04:35,668
And a retired shopkeeper with a pencil

164
00:04:35,668 --> 00:04:36,843
noticed first.

